+All 54 pre-existing unit tests still pass. Liveness was verified by
+temporarily inverting the assertion in `csi_param_saturates_at_u16_max`
+(hegel shrank to `value = 0, leading_zeros = 0`) and reverting. An
+exploratory 10x run (`test_cases = 1000` on every property, then reverted)
+found nothing beyond the failures below.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+### Bug 1: C1 controls split across `advance` calls are printed instead of executed
+
+Minimal counterexample (shrunk by hegel): bytes `[0xC2, 0x80]` (UTF-8 for
+`U+0080`), fed as `advance(&[0xC2])` then `advance(&[0x80])`.
+
+- Whole input: `Execute(0x80)`
+- Chunked: `Print('\u{80}')`
+
+Why it's a library bug: `ground_dispatch` deliberately routes decoded
+`'\u{80}'..='\u{9f}'` characters to `Perform::execute` ("Execute a C0 or C1
+control function"), so the same byte stream produces different terminal
+actions depending on where the network/PTY read happens to split — exactly
+the divergence the `partial_utf8` machinery exists to prevent, and the
+behavior the crate's own `partial_utf8*` unit tests pin down for other cases.
+
+Code path at fault: `Parser::advance_partial_utf8` in `src/lib.rs`. Both its
+`Ok(parsed)` branch and its `valid_bytes > 0` branch call
+`performer.print(c)` unconditionally, without the C0/C1 check that
+`ground_dispatch` applies. Two of the added tests fail on this
+(`chunked_advance_equals_whole_input_advance` and
+`print_never_receives_control_chars`); both shrink to `[c2, 80]`.
+
+### Bug 2: `ParamsIter::size_hint` violates the `Iterator` contract when subparams are present
+
+Minimal counterexample (shrunk by hegel): `params.extend(0); params.push(0)`
+— one parameter consisting of two subparameters. `params.iter()` yields
+exactly 1 item, but `size_hint()` returns `(2, Some(2))`.
+
+Why it's a library bug: std documents that `size_hint`'s lower bound must
+not exceed the actual number of remaining items. Consumers such as
+`Vec::from_iter`/`collect` rely on it for pre-allocation, and
+`iter().count()` disagreeing with `size_hint().0` breaks that contract.
+
+Code path at fault: `ParamsIter::size_hint` in `src/params.rs` computes
+`self.params.len() - self.index`, but `Params::len` counts parameters *and*
+subparameters while the iterator yields one item per parameter (each item a
+slice of its subparams). The hint is only correct when no parameter has
+subparameters. (Relatedly, `Params::len`'s doc comment "Returns the number of
+parameters" is inaccurate for the same reason — it returns the total
+including subparameters, as the model test documents.)
+
+Both failing tests were left in place, per instructions; no library code was
+modified.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+None abandoned outright. Two scoping decisions worth recording:
+
+- **CSI/OSC round-trips generate only canonical encodings.** Parameter values
+  are rendered with `to_string()`, totals are kept within `MAX_PARAMS` /
+  `MAX_OSC_PARAMS`, and OSC parameter bytes exclude C0 controls and `;`.
+  These are contract bounds, not test-convenience bounds: outside them the
+  parser is *documented* to be lossy (saturation, `ignore` flag, control
+  bytes ignored inside OSC strings), so an exact round-trip would be an
+  unsound property there. The lossy regions are still covered by the
+  saturation, size-limit, and no-panic properties.
+- **The chunk-equivalence property was expected to be possibly unsound** for
+  invalid UTF-8 (the comment in `advance_ground` about skipping invalid bytes
+  hints chunked and whole processing might legitimately differ). Tracing the
+  code showed replacement-character emission is actually consistent; the only
+  divergence found is Bug 1, which contradicts documented `execute`
+  behavior, so the property was kept at full strength.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+Most things worked exactly as documented; the failures shrank to textbook
+minimal counterexamples, and the imperative draw style made the chunking and
+resume-loop tests easy to express. Specific notes:
+
+1. **The MSRV gotcha in the Rust reference paid off immediately.**
+   `cargo add --dev hegeltest` failed against `rust-version = 1.62.1` with
+   precisely the error the Setup section predicts, and
+   `--ignore-rust-version` worked. No time lost.
+
+2. **Gap: `#[hegel::test]` does not compile in `#![no_std]` crates without an
+   extra import.** vte is `no_std` when built with `--no-default-features`,
+   and the crate root already has `#[cfg(all(test, not(feature = "std"))))]
+   extern crate std;`. That is not enough: the macro expansion calls
+   `.to_string()` on `&str`, which needs the `ToString` *trait in scope*, and
+   a `no_std` crate has no std prelude — so every `#[hegel::test]` fails with
+   E0599 "no method named `to_string`". Fix is a one-liner in the test module
+   (`use std::string::ToString;`), but it took a failed build to discover.
+   The reference's Setup section covers edition-2015 `extern crate hegel;`
+   but says nothing about `no_std` crates; vte's category (`no-std`) suggests
+   this will recur. Suggested addition next to the edition-2015 note: "In
+   `#![no_std]` crates, test builds need both `extern crate std` and `use
+   std::string::ToString;` in the test module, because the macro expansion
+   calls `.to_string()` outside the std prelude."
+
+3. **Minor reference gap: no `char`/codepoint generator.** The generator
+   reference has text/binary but nothing for single codepoints. For UTF-8
+   boundary testing I wanted "arbitrary `char` with the C1 control range
+   boosted" and built it as `one_of!(integers::<u32>() ranges) .. char::from_u32`
+   by hand. A `generators::chars()` (or a documented idiom in the reference,
+   like the bignum digit-string idiom) would have saved the detour, and
+   ASCII-only `text()` habits are exactly what the skill warns about.
+
+4. **The "boundary conjunctions need help" paragraph in Generator Discipline
+   was load-bearing.** Bug 1 requires a C1 codepoint *and* a chunk split
+   inside its 2-byte encoding. With the C1 range boosted via `one_of!` the
+   failure reproduces at the default 100 cases; with plain
+   `generators::binary()` the conjunction is a ~10^-3 coincidence per case
+   and the committed test would likely be flaky-green. The skill text told me
+   to do this before I knew the bug existed.
+
+5. **Stateful testing section was accurate** (rules with `tc.assume` guards,
+   invariants running on the initial state, `hegel::stateful::run` consuming
+   the machine). The `Params` machine worked first try.
+
+6. **Practical friction, not skill's fault but worth knowing:** hegel's dep
+   tree (hegeltest-c with cbindgen, clap, dashu, etc.) adds ~230 MB of debug
+   build artifacts. On this space-constrained box the first link failed with
+   ENOSPC; `CARGO_PROFILE_DEV_DEBUG=0 CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0` kept it workable.
+   A one-line note in Setup ("the first build compiles a sizable native
+   helper crate") would set expectations.
+
+7. **Liveness-check and 10x-run advice worked as written**, including gotcha
+   13 (no global case-count override): `sed`-ing `test_cases = 1000` in and
+   out was mildly clunky but fine. The deliberate liveness failure left a
+   `.hegel/` database entry as gotcha 11 warns; deleted, and `.hegel/` added
+   to `.gitignore` per gotcha 3.
